WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:23.510
THE STATE OF ADBLOCK:
Back in 2012, AdBlock cost Google $887 million.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.420
By 2015, this number rose to $6.6 billion.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:35.940
And by 2020, AdBlock was causing publishers
to lose $35 billion worth of ad revenue.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.400
But it doesn’t seem like Google cares all
that much.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.430
In fact, the most popular AdBlock is offered
through the Chrome Webstore itself with hundreds

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:50.500
of thousands of reviews and a total of 350
million downloads.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.390
You would think that any reasonable business
would have taken this down by now, or at least

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.440
taken steps to make AdBlock ineffective.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.949
I mean, AdBlock surely has no chance against
Google’s tens of thousands of engineers

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:03.949
right?

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:09.140
Well, the answer is yes and a perfect example
of this is the YouTube app.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.590
Due to the design of mobile apps, no AdBlock
can block ads on the YouTube app itself.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.049
But, there is a way around this on mobile
devices.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:21.659
Instead of using the YouTube app, you can
use a mobile browser along with AdBlock.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:27.390
So, while Google has added roadblocks along
the way, they haven’t gone as far as completely

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.840
taking down AdBlock even though they could
theoretically do it quite easily.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.020
In fact, they have not only not taken steps
to eliminate AdBlock, but Google has actually

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.960
been supporting AdBlock.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.810
They’ve been paying AdBlock tens of millions
every year to whitelist ads.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.920
And this is how Adblock makes much of their
revenue to remain operational.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:54.150
Hearing this, you would probably think that
Google has come to accept AdBlock as an unavoidable

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:58.030
loss in revenue similar to piracy within the
media industry.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:03.430
But, what if I told you that AdBlock is beneficial
for Google.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.480
It actually increases their click through
rates, conversion rates, and even ad rates.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.350
It also allows them to maintain their domineering
monopoly within the browser space and the

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.580
search space.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.050
And if you don’t believe me, just listen
to this.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:23.710
Back in 2017, Google was trying to build their
own AdBlock that would be built into Chrome.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:30.099
So, here’s Google has not only not taken
down AdBlock, but why they pay AdBlock tens

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:33.950
of millions and support the overall effort.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.099
UNSTOPPABLE MONOPOLY:
First of all, Google isn’t stupid.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:44.209
They’re not gonna support AdBlock if they
think that it’s an net loss for the company.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:49.410
The reality is that allowing AdBlock is more
beneficial for Google than blocking it and

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:53.409
one of the main reasons for this is that it
allows them to protect their monopoly.

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:59.000
Here’s the thing, Chrome is by far the most
popular browser in the world with over 60%

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.040
marketshare across all devices.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.161
But, if you look closer, you’ll see that
Chrome is the only browser that’s backed

00:03:06.161 --> 00:03:08.739
by an ad business.

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:13.130
Safari is backed by Apple who is mainly a
hardware and services business.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:18.019
They do have plans about entering the Ad space,
but that has yet to happen in a substantial

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:19.019
manner.

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:23.520
As for Edge, well, Edge is backed by Microsoft
who basically doesn’t even need consumer

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.140
based revenue anymore as they’ve transitioned
to being an enterprise business.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.760
And finally, as for the last notable player,
we have Firefox who is a non profit who’s

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.650
actually staying afloat thanks to donations
from Google.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.459
If you’re wondering why in the world Google
pays Firefox, be sure to check out our video

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:46.569
on that, but getting back to the browsers,
Chrome is the only browser thats dependent

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:47.689
on ad based revenue.

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:48.830
This probably doesn’t sound like a big deal.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.239
Google’s an ad business, and they make most
of their money through ads, that’s not surprising.

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:54.209
And you’re right, that’s not surprising,
but it does make them vulnerable.

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:58.720
Just think about, what happens if Google stopped
allowing AdBlock on Chrome?

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.970
Well, AdBlock suddenly becomes a selling feature
for everyone else, and given that none of

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:03.970
them need ad revenue, you can bet that they’ll
sell the crap out of this.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.500
Of course, they’re not gonna just publicly
tell you that you should switch to Safari

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.239
because it has AdBlock.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:09.880
That would probably be bad PR.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:10.880
But, they’ll use more subtle avenues to
make sure that you know.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.829
They might make a public deal with AdBlock
or feature the AdBlock app on the app store

00:04:15.829 --> 00:04:19.060
or pay journalists to make a comparison between
the browsers.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:24.290
All of this would result in much of the 350
million users who currently have AdBlock to

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.050
consider or even switch to one of these other
browsers.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:33.130
But, by making AdBlock easily accessible on
Chrome, Google is able to protect this massive

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.700
segment of the market.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.830
AdBlock isn’t just effective at fending
of large players either.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.820
It’s also effective at fending of new up
and coming players as well.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.610
All of the browsers that we just discussed
were created by established companies as an

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.460
afterthought and that’s why they don’t
need ads.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:50.490
If you were gonna create a browser based company
though, it’s very likely that it would be

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.490
centered around ads.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.970
And if you wanna have any chance at taking
on Chrome, you too would have to allow AdBlock.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.800
But, while that’s sustainable for Google
with their nearly $300 billion in revenue,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.759
it’s unlikely that it would be sustainable
for you.

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:07.430
So, from Google’s perspective, allowing
AdBlock does indeed mean sacrificing a lot

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:13.590
of revenue, but the alternative is sacrificing
marketshare which is a lot more valuable.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.930
AD PERFORMANCE:
Aside from protecting marketshare, AdBlock

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.230
actually allows Google to have far better
ad performance.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:27.220
Here’s the thing, what type of person is
gonna go out of their way to get the AdBlock

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.789
extension or use safari to watch YouTube instead
of the YouTube app?

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:36.490
Well, the answer is someone who really doesn’t
want to see ads.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:43.050
Ironically, this is almost half of the internet
as 42.7% of the world uses some form of AdBlock.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:48.580
Now, Google could force ads on these people,
but given their extreme distaste for ads,

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.770
they would be far less likely to convert which
is not exactly a great thing for Google due

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.820
to how their ad business is structured.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:01.259
In more traditional media like TV and movies,
advertisers pay networks and studios based

00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:02.610
visibility.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.630
This is why it costs $7 million to air a 30
second commercial on the super bowl.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:07.630
How well the ad performs is completely irrelevant
for the broadcaster of the super bowl.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:12.509
They get paid the same amount whether the
ad gets a 1% conversion rate or a 50% conversion

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:13.509
rate.

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:17.630
In other words, the broadcaster doesn’t
promise any sort of performance and likewise

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.660
their compensation isn’t based on performance.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.370
Google also doesn’t promise advertisers
any sort of performance, but here’s the

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:31.430
catch, Google only gets paid if the ad performs
because they use a model called pay per click.

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:36.350
In this model, advertisers bid on how much
money their willing pay per click and the

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.910
person with the highest bid will be displayed
first on search results or YouTube or whatever.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:47.630
So, theoretically, I could bid an insane amount
like $1000 per click and Google could go ahead

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.900
and push that to a billion people.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:56.520
But, if not a single person clicks, I wouldn’t
have to pay Google a single dime.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.870
Conversely, someone could bid a much more
modest $1 per click and the ad could be pushed

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.960
to just 1 million people.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.960
But if this campaign had a 10% click through
rate, this advertiser would have to pay Google

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:09.960
$100,000.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:14.729
As you can see, Google isn’t paid on visibility,
they’re paid on results.

00:07:14.729 --> 00:07:18.970
And so, even though Google doesn’t promise
any sort of results, it’s in their best

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.150
interest that advertisers perform well.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.290
And, one of the easiest ways to do this is
the cut out the people who don’t want to

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.490
see ads.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:30.920
These people would simply be inflating impression
counts, deflating click through rates, and

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:31.920
putting off advertisers.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.030
With that being said, I think the lost revenue
estimates are actaully super inaccurate.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:41.490
I’m not exactly sure how numbers like this
are calculated, but they’re probably using

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:43.930
some sort of basic forecasting model.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.620
If X number of viewers lead to this much ad
revenue, Y number of viewers should lead to

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.340
this much ad revenue.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:55.990
But, for this to be accurate, the ads would
have to perform as well with Y number of viewers

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:01.020
as it did with X number of viewers which as
we discussed in unlikely.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:06.260
If the extra viewers were 10 times less likely
to click, well then, the loss in revenue wouldn’t

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.479
be $35 billion, it would actually be $3.5
billion.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:14.860
And who knows, by focusing on likely ad targets,
Google may have been able to increase ad bids

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:18.229
to a point at which it offsets this cost.

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:25.090
So, AdBlock may not just a marketshare move
for Google, it could straight up be more profitable.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:30.270
CORE BUSINESS PROTECTED:
So far, we’ve been discussing how Google

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:36.210
has been quite accommodating of AdBlock, but
this is not 100% of the story.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:41.320
You see, Google is only accommodating when
we’re talking about revenue that’s auxiliary

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.800
or a bonus.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.430
They’re not actually all that tolerant when
it comes to their core business.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.970
Take search ads for example.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:53.410
Google didn’t waste a second when it came
to striking a deal with AdBlock to white list

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.750
Google search ads.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.760
The same thing can be said about any ad on
any Google based platform.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.269
AdBlock isn’t stopping inbuilt ads on Google
Maps or Gmail or Google Flights or Google

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:07.720
Books or Google Shopping or even mobile YouTube.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.810
Even if you found some AdBlock that accomplishes
this, well, it’s not stopping Google from

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:17.160
continuing to collect data on you and trying
to serve ads through other venues.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.910
So, the bottomline is that Google isn’t
actually ceding in any of their core businesses.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:27.560
The part of the business that they’re ceding
on is the ability to put ads on third party

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.560
websites, and even here they’re only losing
out on the most obtrusive ads that aren’t

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:29.560
whitelisted.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:33.779
If we zoom out, we’ll see that ceding here
isn’t even a loss for Google, it’s actually

00:09:33.779 --> 00:09:38.560
a gain because Google’s most valuable asset
isn’t advertising, it’s attention.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.640
Their number one goal is to consume as much
of your attention as possible and prevent

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.160
you from going onto TikTok or Instagram or
Twitter.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.440
If you go search for an article and all of
the ads on the article are blocked, that’s

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.690
not a big deal.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.310
As long as you’re still researching, you’re
gonna go back to Google, make more searches,

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:01.339
see more sponsored results, and visit more
websites which may have whitelisted ads.

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:06.740
What would be a true loss for Google is if
you visit a website, are bombarded by 10 ads,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:09.860
and decide to go scroll through TikTok instead.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:14.550
This is the main reason that Chrome itself
has an inbuilt adblocker that’s automatically

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.580
enabled.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:20.579
You may have not known this, but Google themselves
block all ads on websites that they deem to

00:10:20.579 --> 00:10:22.720
have a poor ad experiences.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:27.420
This includes sites with too many ads, ads
with flashing graphics and autoplaying audio,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:28.420
and ad walls.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:34.760
In many cases, these ads may be getting displayed
through Google ads, so Google is technically

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:37.149
cutting off their own revenue in the short
term.

00:10:37.149 --> 00:10:41.769
But they’re betting this will lead to a
greater user experience and stronger retention

00:10:41.769 --> 00:10:48.890
which will theoretically be more profitable
over the long term.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:51.410
THE FUTURE OF ADBLOCK:
In the end, Google has taken an extremely

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:54.410
sophisticated approach to AdBlock.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:58.840
Given that they’re an ad business, it would’ve
been easy for them to complain, throw a tantrum,

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.940
and try to eliminate all remnants of AdBlock.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:06.660
But, instead they’ve looked to structure
AdBlock in a way that benefits them.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:11.200
They’ve used AdBlock to protect their market
share and boost their ad performance.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.300
Simultaneously they’ve also worked with
AdBlock to whitelist ads on most of their

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.200
core services other than desktop YouTube.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:23.200
The bottom line is that Google understands
that eliminating AdBlock is not the solution.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:28.019
The solution is to make ads less intrusive,
less annoying, and more appealing.

00:11:28.019 --> 00:11:32.269
The solution is to make the ad experience
so good that people don’t even feel the

00:11:32.269 --> 00:11:38.010
need to use AdBlock even if it’s available,
and that’s really Google ultimate goal.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:41.880
Or atleast, that’s the approach that Google
has been taking so far.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:47.020
With the recession and stagnating ad rates,
Google may backtrack on this decision and

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:50.880
decide to crack down on AdBlock to please
shareholders in the short term.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.930
But, personally, I believe that would be a
huge mistake.

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.149
Google got it right the first time.

00:11:57.149 --> 00:12:02.750
Working through AdBlock is how Google can
truly overcome AdBlock, not be just taking

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:03.930
it down.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.700
And that’s why Google still allows AdBlock.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.279
Do you use AdBlock?

00:12:08.279 --> 00:12:09.620
Comment that down below.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:13.639
Also, drop a like if you hope Google sticks
to this approach.

